/-
OnBytes/Iop/TestLkpToy.lean: OnBytes/Iop/Lkp.lean on the two lookups of a toy key file of the C++
programs. The tests run when this file compiles.

The data. The C++ tool blocks/frame/handover_files.cpp writes a key file for a small made-up
matrix, and with it a list of intermediate values of its verifier. The key's first chain
holds two lookups. This file has, for each of them: its public inputs; its rounds, that is what the
prover sent and what the verifier drew, as the second verifier tests/new/second/key_check.py read
them from the key file; and the values from the C++ verifier's list. firstRounds says how the
data were made. The rounds' names and the words tie, plane, part and key level are those of
OnBytes/Iop/Lkp.lean: its head and Lkp.run explain them.

What is tested. The names of the rounds and the numbers of elements in them are those of
Lkp.roundsOf. The block does not fail on these rounds, and the values that it notes are those of the
C++ verifier, in the same order. What each value depends on: alpha is the draw of the round "LKP";
the public factor depends on alpha and on the lengths and rows of the padding; the first claim on
the table's side on the shown roots of the planes and on the draw "JOIN"; the factor of the leaves
on the challenges of the table's variables in the sumcheck on the leaves; the target of a part's
claim on alpha, on the two values shown at the part's last key level and on the draw "LINE" after
them, and in a part with padding also on that level's challenges of the variables that the padding
fixes.

What is not tested. No compared value depends on a coefficient of a round's polynomial, on the sum
of a hiding polynomial, on a key level before the last, on the table's tree or on the sumcheck on
the bits. These enter the rows alone, and the rows are not tested here: the statement is made up,
with the multiplicities in one row. The order of the two variables in lastKeyLevelOrder does not
show either, only their number.

No module of the program imports this file. It holds no theorem.
-/
import OnBytes.Iop.TestLkp

namespace OnBytes.TestLkpToy

open Lkp

/-- The public inputs of the key's first lookup, taken from the item "ranges" of the key's parameter
bytes (key.one-time.parameters): the sizes and the order as the item has them; the padding is what
the item's runs leave uncovered in each part. readFrom names the message of the made-up statement;
in the key it is another number. -/
def firstPublic : Public where
  parts := [11, 11, 11, 11]
  d := 3
  mu := 4
  live := 14
  readFrom := [0]
  keys := [{ function := some 0 }]
  tables := [{ logRows := 3 }]
  padding := [
    { part := 3, first := 128, length := 896, row := 3 },
    { part := 3, first := 1152, length := 896, row := 3 }]
  lastKeyLevelOrder := [1, 0]

/-- The rounds of the key's first lookup. R: the name of a round; S: an element sent;
D: an element drawn.

How the data were made. The program built from blocks/frame/handover_files.cpp takes a new folder
and nothing else; by its own head all its data come from small numbers in its source. It wrote
key.file (sha256 acf73db919c25f37b9b609d89cc477226d1bc8b31c2b0140d941e203f7a0db6d) and
key.targets.tsv, whose lines with a name that begins with LKP are the values below. Then
tests/new/second/files_check.py with the format pages' folder, the new folder and --write wrote
key.one-time.chain.tsv: for each round of the key's first chain its name, the bytes and the sha256
of the prover's message, and the elements drawn. The first lookup is the rounds 5 to 496 of that
table, the second the rounds 497 to 686. An S line is 24 bytes of the round's message in key.file:
three words of 8 bytes, the low byte first.
Where the tool writes another parameter file, the same steps give
another key file and other elements. -/
